23956298828125, -0.064697265625, -0.007350921630859375, 0.01666259765625, -0.03704833984375, 0.01050567626953125, 0.0207366943359375, 0.033935546875, 0.060302734375, -0.00919342041015625, -0.022491455078125, 0.0030269622802734375, 0.0153045654296875, 0.03466796875, 0.00469207763671875, 0.01580810546875, 0.021331787109375, 0.023040771484375, -0.0269775390625, 0.0416259765625, -0.02032470703125, 0.0157928466796875, -0.0027256011962890625, -0.0391845703125, 0.0266876220703125, 0.01560211181640625, -0.0038318634033203125, -0.0132598876953125, -0.00959014892578125, 0.02178955078125, 0.04620361328125, -0.0141754150390625, -0.019439697265625, 0.0212860107421875, -0.00727081298828125, 0.00290679931640625, -0.04376220703125, -0.02880859375, 0.0302276611328125, 0.0236358642578125, -0.00827789306640625, 0.01043701171875, -0.030792236328125, -0.0093841552734375, -0.044464111328125, -0.0018262863159179688, 0.0248565673828125, 0.0245361328125, -0.0202484130859375, 0.0252227783203125], "payload": {"model": "text-embedding-3-small", "text": "An antibiotic is a type of antimicrobial substance specifically active against bacteria. While \"antimicrobial\" refers broadly to agents that act against various types of microorganisms\u2014including bacteria, viruses, fungi, and protozoa\u2014antibiotics are agents that target bacteria in particular. Antibiotics are commonly used to treat and prevent bacterial infections in humans and animals. They function by either killing bacteria (bactericidal action) or inhibiting their growth (bacteriostatic action). Although the primary use of antibiotics is against bacteria, a few antibiotics, such as metronidazole, also exhibit activity against certain protozoa. However, antibiotics are not effective against viral or fungal infections because viruses and fungi differ significantly from bacteria in their cellular structure and replication mechanisms."}}